From: Gee <gehee@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] h265d: refuse a slice whose PPS names a missing SPS

mpp_hevc_decode_nal_pps() parses a PPS over the one already held under
its id, and on an error only frees it if it was new. A corrupt PPS that
reads a bad pps_sps_id and then fails leaves the held PPS with that id:
an SPS that does not exist, or one past the end of sps_list. The next
slice using that PPS reads sps_list[sps_id] out of range, or passes NULL
to set_sps() and dereferences it - a SIGSEGV in the parser thread, seen
in kestrel-gnd on a damaged air stream.

Check the SPS exists before using it, and drop the slice if not.

Signed-off-by: Gee <gehee@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/mpp/codec/dec/h265/h265d_parser.c
+++ b/mpp/codec/dec/h265/h265d_parser.c
@@ -750,6 +750,14 @@
     }
     s->pps = (HEVCPPS*)s->pps_list[sh->pps_id];
 
+    /* A PPS that failed to re-parse in place keeps whatever sps_id it read,
+     * which need not name an SPS that exists: set_sps(NULL) below. */
+    if (s->pps->sps_id >= MAX_SPS_COUNT || !s->sps_list[s->pps->sps_id]) {
+        mpp_err("PPS %d refers to SPS %d, which does not exist\n",
+                sh->pps_id, s->pps->sps_id);
+        return MPP_ERR_STREAM;
+    }
+
     if (s->sps != (HEVCSPS*)s->sps_list[s->pps->sps_id]) {
         s->sps = (HEVCSPS*)s->sps_list[s->pps->sps_id];
         mpp_hevc_clear_refs(s);
